00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.849 align:start position:0%
over a thousand years of history reside  in the friary of this church in Venice  sacked on over 80 kilometers of shelves  and map manuscript monographs documents  recording generations of Venetian  bureaucracy most of this vast collection  has never been read by modern historians  but a new project aims to uncover the  secrets of this enormous archive and  digitally reconstruct the Venice it  describes  the Venice time-machine project aims to  make the huge amount of data stored in  the State Archives of Venice accessible  to researchers  every page of paperwork must be scanned  and digitized to enable searching and  cross-referencing normal character  recognition software doesn't work well  on the wide variety of handwriting found  in the archive so machine learning is  being used to recognize word shapes and  match them between documents  cross-referencing names means  researchers can create social networks  for the city and the data contained in  maps enables them to recreate the  building up and occasional falling down  of Venice's buildings  information in maps can then be  cross-referenced with historical  paintings as well as modern photos and  combining these images with apparently  mundane paperwork about shops and  businesses means historians can  reconstruct the details of the city at  almost any point in its history this  virtual time machine should allow modern  historians and members of the public to  explore ancient Venice and the written  documents could provide datasets on  anything from trade and financial  markets to public health and the spread  of disease the technology developed for  the venice time machine project could be  applied to other ancient archives to  creating detailed and inspiring windows  into the past  [Music]
 

